  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nToday through next Monday...Weak troughing will exist across \nmainly far eastern portions of the forecast area, as high \npressure aloft begins building into the region from the west. At \nthe surface, a frontal boundary will stall through or just south \nof the region by the end of the day. This, combined with daytime \nheating in the middle 90s, will again result in isolated to \nscattered showers and storms developing across areas mainly along \nand southeast of the Natchez Trace Parkway this afternoon. Heavy \ndownpours and frequent lightning will be possible with storms, \nwith gusty winds possible. As such there is a Marginal Risk for \nisolated severe storms capable of producing damaging wind gusts \nover some central and eastern portions of the CWA this afternoon.\n\nWith high pressure aloft and at the surface becoming more prevalent \nacross the region through the remainder of the forecast period, rain \nchances essentially dwindle to nil.  However in the aforementioned \nfrontal boundary's wake, drier air and less hot conditions will \nexist across the forecast area for the remainder of the week and \ninto the weekend.  As highs Wednesday range from the low to middle \n90s, come late week and into the weekend they'll range from the \nupper 80s to lower 90s.  Lows will likewise follow suit as they'll \nrange from the upper 60s and lower 70s Wednesday morning.  Then \ncooling into the low to middle 60s late in the week and through \nthe weekend. /OAJ/",
